    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away\u2014negotiate.** \n\nThis is a **solid baseline offer** for a Google L6 Senior PM, but you hold significant leverage:\n1. You were **passively recruited** and are happy at Meta.\n2. Google offered **$0 sign-on bonus**, which is standard for an opening bid but leaves easy money on the table.\n3. Your base salary sits around the 60th percentile of their posted range ($183k\u2013$271k).\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & The Math\n\nGoogle standardly uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%):\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | Equity Vest | Total Comp (TC) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $356,400 (33%) | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $356,400 (33%) | **~$638,400** *(+ Refresher 1)* |\n| **Year 3** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $237,600 (22%) | **~$519,600** *(+ Refreshers 1 & 2)* |\n| **Year 4** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $129,600 (12%) | **~$411,600** *(+ Refreshers 1, 2, 3)* |\n| **4-Yr Average** | **$235,000** | **$47,000** | **$270,000/yr** | **~$552,000/yr** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n* **The \"Year 3 Cliff\":** Google\u2019s front-loading makes Years 1 & 2 look very attractive. While annual refreshers (which vest evenly at 25%/year) stack over time, performance ratings dictate refresher sizes, so you absorb risk in Years 3 and 4.\n* **Meta IC6 Parity:** Meta IC6 PM total compensation typically spans **$520,000 to $680,000+** depending on equity appreciation and performance multipliers. If you have unvested Meta RSUs that appreciated, this offer might only be a lateral or slight upgrade in real take-home pay over a 4-year horizon.\n\n---\n\n### What to Negotiate (Specific Target Asks)\n\nAim for a package that pushes your 4-year annualized average closer to **$600k\u2013$650k** and locks in guaranteed upfront cash:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Ask | Justification / Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving unvested Meta equity and forfeited mid-year/annual Meta bonus. Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses to close candidates from Meta. |\n| **Equity (GSUs)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000 \u2013 $1,280,000** | Brings your Year 1/Year 2 stock vest to ~$400k/yr and cushions the Year 3/4 drop. |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000 \u2013 $255,000** | The top of band is $271,000. Asking for ~$250k keeps you within band while boosting your bonus baseline. |\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Negotiation Strategy\n\n#### 1. Express Enthusiasm, But Anchor on Your Current Trajectory\nBecause you were headhunted, you have the psychological advantage. Frame your position around risk and opportunity cost rather than being demanding:\n\n> *\"I\u2019ve really enjoyed meeting the team, and the scope of the L6 role aligns closely with where I want to take my product career. However, because I wasn\u2019t actively looking and have strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, the current compensation isn't quite at the level that would justify making the switch.\"*\n\n#### 2. Address the Front-Loaded Equity & Missing Sign-On\nCall out the compensation structure directly:\n\n> *\"When I look at the 4-year annualized numbers\u2014especially with Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule and the step-down in Years 3 and 4\u2014the average compensation is essentially flat compared to my trajectory at Meta. To offset my unvested equity and make this an easy decision, I am looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$250,000***\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,250,000***\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000\u2013$100,000** to bridge the transition and bonus timing.*\n>\n> *If we can get close to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n#### 3. Do Not Give Hard Numbers on Your Current Meta Comp First\nIf the recruiter asks for your exact W-2 or stock breakdown, keep it high-level:\n* Focus on your **projected 12-to-24 month earnings at Meta** (including refresher grants and bonus expectations).\n* Remind them that moving companies resets your internal social capital and performance baseline, which carries risk that must be priced into the new package.",
